WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.760
 In this video, I've got two things

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.680
 I want to try to accomplish.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.480
 I've been getting a lot of comments

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.740
 on a couple different fronts.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.040
 One of them is people want to talk

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.800
 about book recommendations because...

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.340
 I have a few of them [books].

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.260
 And the second is that there's been,

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.000
 I know it's been tongue-in-cheek,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.740
 but there's some

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.540
 question about whether or not I'm

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.500
 actually an AI, which creates kind

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.780
 of an academic question

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.080
 about, "so, how would you prove

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:28.800
 whether someone's really real or an

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:29.380
 AI?"

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.430
 And I think I might have had an

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.980
 idea about how I might be able to

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.460
 create a situation that

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.940
 I don't think an AI could pull off.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.700
 So let's try to do that.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.870
 Hopefully once this is done, you'll

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:42.025
 be convinced that

00:00:42.025 --> 00:00:43.858
 I-I-I-I'm re-re-re-re-re-re...

00:00:52.857 --> 00:00:54.340
 This is the Internet of Bugs.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.000
 My name is Carl, and today we're

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.710
 going to talk about, or I'm going

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:58.980
 to talk about, the top

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:01.480
 10 books that have been valuable to

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.530
 me over the course of my career,

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:04.820
 from a programming

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:05.820
 standpoint.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.570
 You'll notice kind of a theme in

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.510
 these, I think, but let me go grab

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:10.140
 them.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.280
 I'm going to move this over here,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:16.530
 and hopefully as I roll over here,

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.700
 and I take these books

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.210
 off and show them to you, and set

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.590
 them down here on the table, it

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.100
 will convince you that

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:33.290
 an AI would not be able to pull off

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:38.660
 all of this moving around in all of

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.020
 these book things

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.360
 and keeping all the covers

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:43.820
 consistent, and keeping me

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.660
 consistent, and keeping my shirt

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.970
 consistent, and all of that kind of

00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:48.660
 stuff.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.970
 Hopefully, at the end of this, it

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:57.960
 will become apparent to you that it's

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.820
 not guaranteed that

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.980
 I'm real, but at least if they were

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.760
 going to do an AI of this, or if

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.300
 they were going

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.870
 to do a fake computer generator in

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.710
 one of this, it would require like,

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:14.180
 you know, Marvel

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.070
 level 3D full model kind of things,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.940
 and it's just completely infeasible

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:20.700
 that anyone 

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.480
 would want to do that for a silly

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.510
 YouTube channel, so hopefully that

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:26.580
 convinces you.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.680
 Now it's on to the part of the

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.190
 video where we talk about the

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:32.540
 actual books.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.800
 All right, so I want to start with

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:35.740
 this book.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.730
 This is "The Pragmatic Programmer" by

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.060
 Hunt and Thomas.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:41.950
 I will put links to all of these in

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.970
 the show at the bottom, so don't

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:45.020
 worry about trying

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:46.890
 to have to write down from looking

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:48.220
 at them on the screens.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.320
 This book is about managing your

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.860
 career as a programmer.

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:55.430
 It's got some stuff about the

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:57.210
 techniques of programming, but it's

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.220
 mainly about kind of

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.060
 the philosophy of programming, and

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.520
 it's kind of about how you decide

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:04.860
 what's going to be

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.300
 important to you and how you make

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.150
 decisions and how you kind of curate

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:12.620
 the projects that

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:15.780
 you work on over your career.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.410
 If you don't take anything else

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:19.170
 away from this, this is probably

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.340
 the most useful book

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.880
 for you if you're coming out of

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.780
 college or you're early in your

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:29.060
 career and you're looking

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:32.910
 at "What should I be doing over the

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:37.190
 next few years?" this is probably my

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:40.460
 first recommendation.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:41.430
 You've probably heard me talk about

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:41.780
 this before.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.850
 This is "The Mythical Man-Month,"

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:46.300
 by Fred Brooks.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.960
 This kind of, along with "The

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.440
 Pragmatic Programmer," these are the

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.340
 kinds of things that are more

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:53.620
 or less AI-proof.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:56.700
 This book is about the humanity.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.350
 This book is about the people part

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.240
 of software engineering, and so a

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.180
 lot of the things about

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:05.980
 how getting requirements and

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:07.700
 arguing about things and how

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.300
 projects get managed.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.390
 Those are the kinds of things that

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:11.620
 this book is about, and I don't see

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:12.300
 those things getting

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.930
 changed much by AI regardless of

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:14.820
 what happens.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.010
 This is a thing that's going to be

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:19.460
 useful to know over your career.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:21.100
 This book, "Working Effectively with

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.310
 Legacy Code," I have a whole video

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:23.180
 where I talk about

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:24.580
 this a lot.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:27.360
 I'll link that, but this is a book,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.980
 the best book that I've ever found

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.380
 on software testing,

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:32.320
 which is kind of sad because it's

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:33.440
 about 20 years old and there hasn't

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:34.060
 been a book written

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:35.340
 in the last 20 years that's better

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.500
 about software testing, which is

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:37.100
 kind of sad, but

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.940
 this book is actually really good.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:42.230
 This is going to be an unpopular

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:42.820
 one I think.

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:46.580
 This is "SQL for Smarties."

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:47.740
 There's a whole series of these.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.450
 This is the first one, the basic

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.630
 one on basic SQL stuff, but SQL-

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.820
 basically everything that

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:56.980
 you really care about lives on SQL.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:59.300
 Your bank account lives on SQL, the

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.460
 title to your house lives on SQL,

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:02.580
 or the title to

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.640
 the apartment that you live in is in

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.870
 SQL, your taxes are all in SQL, the

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:10.780
 whole world pretty

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:12.140
 much.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:14.080
 The vast majority of the data lives

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:16.020
 in SQL, and you're going to have to

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:16.940
 deal with it.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:17.940
 It's just a thing.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:20.760
 It's not that hard, and getting it

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.260
 to perform well is important.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:25.220
 There are a lot of people that just

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:26.100
 use the ORM.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.920
 They use the library that makes

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.810
 their code talk to SQL, and they

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:31.100
 don't ever look under

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:32.420
 the covers at what SQL actually

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:32.820
 does.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:34.230
 I recommend you look under the

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:34.820
 covers.

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:37.020
 There's a lot of really interesting

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.300
 stuff there that's very useful.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.940
 This is not necessarily the book

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:41.540
 for you.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:43.360
 This is the book I learned 

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:44.580
 Assembler from.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.460
 These days, it would be an x64.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:48.210
 This is back in the old old days

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.590
 when x86 computers weren't x64

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:51.860
 computers, and we could

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.800
 only have one megabyte of addressable

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:54.380
 memory.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:55.380
 It was a whole thing.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:56.970
 Anyway, I don't necessarily think

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.650
 you ought to read this book, but I

00:05:58.650 --> 00:05:59.340
 think you ought to

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:00.540
 learn about assembler.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:03.420
 The reason for that is there will

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:06.410
 come a time when you have a problem

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:07.740
 that you need to

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.270
 understand, and if you're not the

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:12.950
 person who can dig into the assembler

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:13.900
 and figure out

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:15.640
 what's going on, at least having a

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:17.320
 working understanding of what it

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:18.460
 means, then someone

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:19.710
 else is going to have to do that

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:21.060
 for you, and that's not going to

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:21.940
 look good for you.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:23.540
 When you've got your compiler has

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.030
 made a mistake and it's tried to

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:26.100
 optimize something

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.560
 in its inline to function that wasn't

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:28.860
 supposed to inline or that kind of

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:29.860
 thing, understanding

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.310
 the assembler of the platform that

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:32.860
 you're looking at, not necessarily

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:33.860
 to be able to write

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:35.170
 a bunch of assembler code, but to

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:36.280
 at least be able to read it and

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:37.260
 understand it when you

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.990
 look at what the compiler or what

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:40.520
 the interpreter spit out is a

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:41.580
 valuable skill.

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:43.580
 Like I said, you're going to notice

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:44.500
 a theme here.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.820
 This is the "Introduction to Algorithms"

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:48.140
 class, second edition.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:49.900
 This is the one in the video where

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:51.640
 I talked about my interviews with

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:52.260
 Amazon.

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:54.260
 This is the one that I read from

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:56.800
 and worked the homework assignments

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:57.940
 in, the one that

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:00.080
 the MIT Intro to Algorithms class

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:01.500
 was using at the time.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.140
 Actually, I think they were using the

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:04.660
 third edition is the second.

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:06.230
 I don't really care about this book,

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:07.620
 particularly there are a bunch of

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:08.660
 different algorithms

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:10.590
 books, it doesn't really matter

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:12.200
 which one you get as long as it's

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:13.260
 one of the handful

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.140
 of popular ones, but get one.

00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:16.760
 I don't recommend that you memorize

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:17.380
 stuff in it.

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:19.820
 I don't memorize stuff in it except

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:21.930
 right when I'm cramming for an

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.280
 interview, but you

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.410
 want to be able to look stuff up in

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:26.960
 it and I recommend that

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.640
 periodically you do.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:30.250
 I don't think that pulling an

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.250
 algorithm out of the thin air and

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:33.820
 coding it and then expecting

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:35.940
 it to work makes any sense at all.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:37.400
 So look the thing up, that's the

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:38.530
 right thing to do and having a

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:39.860
 thing where you can actually

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:41.100
 go look it up is important.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.120
 So get yourself an algorithms book,

00:07:44.120 --> 00:07:46.340
 whichever one you pick.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:48.370
 This is "Transaction Processing" by

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:49.100
 Jim Gray.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:50.500
 I don't know that there's another

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:51.620
 book that's like this.

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:53.780
 This is the only one that I know of.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:55.400
 This is the book that you use when

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:56.760
 you're doing complicated

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:57.740
 transactions.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.370
 If you're doing banking, you're

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:00.670
 doing three-phase commits, that

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:01.320
 kind of stuff.

00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:04.190
 Get this book or if there's a new

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:06.950
 version of it or by the time you

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:08.840
 get to it, they've

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:09.800
 written another book.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:11.770
 But transactions are a thing you

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:13.940
 don't want to screw around with.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:16.630
 The people that write these are

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:19.610
 really good at making sure that you

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:21.440
 really know whether

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:23.860
 the transaction actually committed

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:25.890
 or if it got rolled back and if you're

00:08:25.890 --> 00:08:26.800
 doing things

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:28.310
 that involve money or you're doing

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:29.620
 things that involve medical

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:30.640
 information or anything

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:31.640
 like that.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:32.820
 It's very important that the

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:33.740
 information gets captured or you

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:35.100
 know that it didn't get captured.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.270
 So don't mess around with

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:38.170
 transaction processing stuff if you're

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:39.860
 going to be doing transactions.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:41.430
 And if you're never going to do

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:43.220
 transactions through your entire

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:44.500
 career, I think you're

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:45.920
 going to have a very fluffy career.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:47.180
 So I think you ought to at least do

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:48.180
 a few projects that have

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.400
 transactions in them because

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:49.760
 otherwise

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:51.160
 I'm not sure what you're trying to

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:51.840
 accomplish.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:53.640
 Get this book or a book like it.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:54.850
 And when you get to a point where

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:56.290
 you're doing transactions, get the

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:57.280
 book out, dust it off

00:08:57.280 --> 00:08:58.330
 and make sure that you're doing

00:08:58.330 --> 00:08:58.880
 them right.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:08:59.880
 It's important.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:02.700
 Okay, this is "TCP/IP illustrated

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:06.120
 volume one" by W Richard Stevens.

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:07.400
 You'll see him again in a minute.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:10.790
 This book is a book that talks

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:14.540
 about how the protocols that make

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:17.120
 the internet run work.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:18.760
 And the book I actually learned

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:20.620
 them from first was Comer, which is

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:21.400
 not great.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:22.400
 There are three volumes of this.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.140
 This is the volume one is the one

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:24.960
 you really need.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.140
 The thing that this book does that

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:29.010
 the books before it didn't do is

00:09:29.010 --> 00:09:30.320
 this book gives you

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:32.310
 an understanding of what the

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:34.640
 packets on the wire are doing from

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.340
 the point of view of looking

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.840
 at the packets.

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:40.650
 So when you're trying to figure out

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:41.830
 what's going on and you don't

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:43.040
 understand what's going

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.510
 on and something weird is happening,

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:47.750
 use whatever tools your operating

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:48.760
 system has.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:50.720
 Look at the raw packets on the wire

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:52.470
 between your application and

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:53.680
 whoever it is that it's

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.220
 talking to and go through and look

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:56.530
 at those and you'll be able to

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:59.040
 figure out, okay, what

00:09:59.040 --> 00:09:59.980
 I thought was happening is not

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:00.800
 actually happening.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.230
 This is actually the actual truth

00:10:02.230 --> 00:10:03.450
 of what's going on between my

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:04.720
 application and the client

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:07.030
 or the server that I'm talking to

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:08.880
 or that kind of thing.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:10.840
 Learning how to debug things from

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:12.780
 the point of view of getting the

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:14.000
 information from the

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:16.340
 packets on the network wire using

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:18.860
 TCP dump or whatever it is you're

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:20.200
 doing is an invaluable

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:22.290
 skill when all else fails and you

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.880
 just don't know what is going wrong.

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.930
 That's usually where I start my

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:30.850
 investigation into where a weird

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:32.480
 bug happened.

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:34.890
 Another W. Richard Stevens book,

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.200
 this is "Advanced Programming in the

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:38.720
 Unix Environment."

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.230
 This is the same kind of thing as

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:43.920
 the last book except this is about

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:45.600
 Unix system calls.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:48.010
 And if your code is running on top

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:50.430
 of Linux, even if it's running in a

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:51.840
 sub process under

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.180
 nginx on top of Linux in a

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:57.710
 virtual environment, whatever, if

00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:00.240
 you trace your process and you

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.590
 use Dtrace or you use Strace or

00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:03.030
 you use whatever it is that your

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:05.520
 operating system has and you

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:08.180
 go through and you see exactly what

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.690
 calls your code is making and what

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:11.920
 files it's opening

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:13.330
 and that kind of thing, that's a

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:14.760
 place when you don't know where

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:15.760
 else to go, actually

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:17.110
 trace your code and see exactly

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:18.450
 what it's doing in terms of what

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:19.680
 resources it's accessing

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:21.360
 and that kind of thing.

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:22.570
 You don't necessarily need to use

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:23.060
 this one.

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:26.050
 There's an equivalent one of these

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:28.930
 for Linux, but make sure you have

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:30.680
 some way of digging

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:32.360
 in and understanding what the

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:33.900
 system calls that exist in the

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:35.440
 system that you're running

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:36.860
 on top of because if something

00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:38.630
 weird is happening and you debug it,

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:40.200
 this is the kind of information

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:41.200
 you're going to have.

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:42.520
 You don't need to memorize this cover to

00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:42.960
 cover.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:44.250
 You don't need to do that with the

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:45.440
 TCP/IP one either, but you need to

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:46.240
 be familiar enough

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:47.450
 with it that when something goes

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:48.760
 wrong, you can dig the book out.

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:49.760
 You can open it up.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:50.760
 You can flip through it.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:51.680
 You can find the part that's

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:52.800
 relevant and you can debug whatever

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:53.520
 it is that the problem

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:54.520
 is.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:57.840
 All right, so last book:

00:11:57.840 --> 00:11:59.100
 This is "Firewalls and Internet

00:11:59.100 --> 00:11:59.740
 Security."

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:01.800
Bellovin."
We refer to this as "Cheswick and

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.400
 This book was written in 1994.

00:12:04.400 --> 00:12:05.750
 This is not directly going to be

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.160
 relevant to you, but let me tell

00:12:07.160 --> 00:12:08.080
 you why I'm talking

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:09.080
 to you about it.

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:12.270
 When I graduated from college and I

00:12:12.270 --> 00:12:15.460
 got my first job, Firewalls were

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:16.840
 not a thing.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:18.070
 There was some open source stuff

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:19.000
 that you could use to try to

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:19.840
 protect yourself from

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:21.900
 the internet, but there was no

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:23.970
 firewall product you could really

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:25.200
 buy at the time.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:27.280
 This was one of the very first

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:29.230
 books that explained from some

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:31.040
 researchers at AT&amp;T what

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:32.890
 they did to protect their network,

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:34.700
 how they protected their network,

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:35.760
 what they had, you

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:37.310
 know, the things that they did, the

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:38.810
 principles that they worked on when

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:39.520
 they were trying

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:40.990
 to secure their network, that kind

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:41.520
 of thing.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:44.220
 This book, I would say, launched

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:46.360
 the firewall industry.

00:12:46.360 --> 00:12:47.730
 It would have happened at some

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:49.300
 point anyway, but this was kind of

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:50.520
 the kicking off point.

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:51.830
 The reason that I'm telling you

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:53.080
 this is not because I think you

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:54.400
 need to know how Firewalls

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:57.400
 work, although it can be useful,

00:12:57.400 --> 00:13:00.320
 but because this is a book that was

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:01.960
 about a topic that

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:04.550
 nobody really understood at the

00:13:04.550 --> 00:13:06.920
 time that the book came out.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.010
 By getting this book at the time

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:10.980
 and by learning about how this book

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:12.400
 worked at the time and

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:14.110
 reading it and really, I don't know

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:15.580
 if you could tell, but I've read

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:16.360
 the hell out of

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:19.060
 this book, by becoming very

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:22.030
 familiar with a thing that was a

00:13:22.030 --> 00:13:24.320
 state of the art kind of

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:26.470
 feature in programming and at the

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:28.410
 time that it came out, it was an

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:29.840
 incredibly valuable

00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:31.250
 skill for me to know and a lot of

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:32.930
 folks that never bothered to learn

00:13:32.930 --> 00:13:33.880
 how networks and

00:13:33.880 --> 00:13:35.010
 firewalls and that kind of stuff

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:36.160
 worked missed out on a whole lot of

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:36.960
 opportunities that

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:39.200
 I had because I learned it.

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:41.560
 Firewalls are not going to be your

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:43.980
 thing because that's pretty much

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:46.120
 baked, but whatever's

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:49.000
 important at a given time,

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.640
 especially if you're early in your

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:55.400
 career, find the thing that

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:56.940
 nobody really understands or very

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:58.590
 few people understand. That might

00:13:58.590 --> 00:13:59.480
 be AI, that might

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:02.470
 be LLMs, that might be, you know,

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:04.320
 at one point in time it was

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:05.640
 blockchain.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:07.160
 No telling what it is, it's

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:08.850
 probably not going to be relevant

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:11.400
 forever, but find those things

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:13.810
 that are popular, find those things

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:15.970
 that people need help with, find

00:14:15.970 --> 00:14:17.320
 good books on how they

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:20.680
 work and read them, even if you don't

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:23.840
 necessarily work just in that area.

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:26.660
 Being able to talk intelligently

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:29.850
 and understand how things work, the

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:31.680
 guts of how things work

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:32.980
 and to be able to have

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:35.580
 conversations intelligently about

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:37.140
 how they work is incredibly

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:37.840
 valuable

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:40.110
 during the point of the curve of a

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:43.080
 particular adoption of a technology

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:44.160
 that not everybody

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:45.520
 just takes for granted that that

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:46.940
 technology exists and everybody

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:47.920
 knows how to use it.

00:14:47.920 --> 00:14:50.450
 I said earlier I thought you would

00:14:50.450 --> 00:14:53.250
 see a theme, it's important to me,

00:14:53.250 --> 00:14:54.360
 it's been important

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:55.410
 to me my whole career and I

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:56.810
 recommend that it's important to

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:57.160
 you.

00:14:57.160 --> 00:14:58.670
 The vast majority of people that

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:00.360
 are doing software are not going to

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:00.880
 do this.

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:04.110
 My recommendation to you is seek

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:07.100
 out the resources that let you

00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:10.800
 understand not just what you're doing,

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:13.800
 but how the thing that you're doing

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:16.040
 works and why it works.

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:17.650
 Look for the resources that talk

00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:18.980
 about how the packets are

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:20.160
 structured, about how the

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:23.350
 system calls are made, at how the

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:25.160
 assembler is run.

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:27.450
 Look at the underlying base level

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:29.900
 of what's happening in the computer

00:15:29.900 --> 00:15:31.360
 and then understand

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:33.350
 how whatever it is you're working

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.320
 on at the application layer hooks

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:36.240
 into that.

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.180
 If you can do that, then you're

00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:40.100
 going to be miles ahead of most of

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:42.120
 the folks that really

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:44.290
 are just working on the surface and

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:46.000
 they don't have the ability to dive

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:47.000
 deep into what's

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:48.660
 actually happening in the event

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.260
 that something goes wrong and at

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:51.560
 the point where something

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:53.000
 goes wrong-and remember that the

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:54.770
 Internet is full of Bugs. At some

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:55.800
 point something's going

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:56.800
 to go wrong.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:58.360
 At the point that something goes

00:15:58.360 --> 00:15:59.350
 wrong, that's where you can

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:00.760
 differentiate yourself because

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:03.680
 you hopefully have taken the time

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:06.280
 to learn about the foundation of

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:08.000
 what the stuff that

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:09.820
 you're writing is running on and

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:11.340
 you'll be able to dig in and figure

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:12.320
 out what's going

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:13.810
 on in a way that the folks that

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:15.570
 have only ever bothered to look at

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:16.560
 Python or only ever

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:19.210
 bothered to look at Node don't

00:16:19.210 --> 00:16:21.910
 understand and they're not going to

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:23.200
 be able to solve

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:24.380
 those problems and you're not

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:25.610
 necessarily going to be able to

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:26.640
 solve the problems, but

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:28.090
 you're going to be able to move the

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:29.600
 problems along to the point that in

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:30.480
 conjunction with

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.100
 the other folks on the other teams,

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.680
 you'll be able to get it solved.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:35.840
 That's an incredibly valuable skill.

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:37.360
 It never shows up on an interview

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:38.440
 pretty much.

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:40.380
 You don't ever see it in a job

00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:41.790
 description, but if you want to be

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:42.960
 the person on the team

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:44.840
 that's valuable, having some

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:46.760
 understanding of what's happening

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:48.200
 underneath the surface

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:49.590
 of where your code is actually

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:51.210
 running is the most valuable skill

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:52.360
 I think you can have.

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:54.070
 So that's what I have for you today,

00:16:54.070 --> 00:16:55.600
 hopefully that was useful.

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:56.810
 There are going to be more, I'm

00:16:56.810 --> 00:16:58.040
 going to call them "Book Club" I

00:16:58.040 --> 00:16:59.360
 think, more Book Club episodes

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:00.800
 coming up that are going to be

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:02.320
 talking about more stuff.

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:03.900
 One of the reasons that I got the

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:05.750
 older stuff, the more recent a book

00:17:05.750 --> 00:17:06.760
 is that I have that

00:17:06.760 --> 00:17:08.830
 I can recommend, the more likely it

00:17:08.830 --> 00:17:10.790
 is that that book I'm going to have

00:17:10.790 --> 00:17:11.800
 electronically

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:13.560
 and not as paper.

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:14.770
 So since people actually wanted to

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:16.050
 talk about the paper bookshelves, I

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:17.760
 went ahead and made

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:19.300
 a video first about books that I

00:17:19.300 --> 00:17:21.080
 was sure were going to be on paper.

00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:23.010
 So this has been the first Dead

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:25.010
 Tree edition of the Internet of

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:26.240
 Bugs Book Club.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:28.140
 I hope it was useful to you. Take

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:30.250
 care of yourself. Let's be careful

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:31.160
 out there.

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:32.160
 Thanks for watching.

